            <ArticleTitle>Aptamer-modified selenium nanoparticles for dark-field microscopy imaging of nucleolin.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>13047-13050</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1039/c7cc07664a</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Selenium nanoparticles (SeNPs) with strong green scattering light serving as light scattering nanoprobes for dark-field microscopy imaging are developed for the first time. The SeNPs show good stability, excellent biocompatibility, and high light-producing power, and can precisely and specifically target cancer cells with overexpressed nucleolin (NCL) after modification with aptamers, revealing that the SeNPs can act as a good light scattering nanoprobe for bioimaging and biolabeling.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
